  "experience": {
    "narrative": "You're seated at Darling Harbour Seafood Emporium, where everything is designed for tourists who don't know better. The menu comes in eight languages, laminated and photo-heavy. The Seafood tower arrives looking professional but tasting disappointingly generic—competent execution without soul, ingredients chosen for cost rather than quality. Looking around, you notice you're surrounded entirely by other tourists, all making the same mistake. The locals walking by outside don't even glance in—they know better. You finish the meal feeling like you've wasted an opportunity, chosen convenience and hype over the real thing that's probably two streets over.",
    "rating": 2.3
  }
